commit 677c7efb299df36619aafa38b9afdf75c1faff91

C03: a weather question searches with the person's own weather words and matches the forecast's synonyms (WEB1445)

Local weather requests («qué clima hace hoy», «mostrame el clima», «va a llover mañana») have been conditioned since WEB1269/1271: the engine answers any weather query with the local forecast, but the query carried the request verbs («mostrame», «hace») and its accent-folded form, and the relevance filter demanded every token in the result while forecasts say «tiempo», not «clima».

baxy_mind effect_intent: the live weather lookups pass the person's text with its accents as evidence; _weather_lookup_query strips courtesy, request verbs, the interrogative scaffold («qué clima hace», «cómo está el», «is it going to», «will it») and «en google/internet», keeping the weather words in the person's order («clima hoy», «clima», «va a llover mañana», «weather today»); a definition («qué es el clima») is left to the previous path. __main__ grounds web.search with that query.
Baxy.Providers.Windows WebBrowserAdapter.IsSearchResultRelevant: a query token is also satisfied by a member of its weather family (clima/tiempo/weather/pronóstico/forecast, lluvia/llover/rain, mañana/tomorrow, temperatura/temperature).

Live probe against the engine from this PC: «clima hoy», «clima», «va a llover mañana» and «weather today» return local forecasts that now pass the filter; a named city («clima en Buenos Aires») still returns the local forecast and stays rejected as irrelevant, which is the truthful outcome. Providers.Windows and App recompiled (BUILD1445). Tests not run (owner order).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>


